1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,162
Chapter 55: Thromboembolic Disorders.

3
00:00:06,162 --> 00:00:29,387
Pathophysiology Thrombophilias Antiphospholipid Syndrome Thrombophilia Screening Deep-Vein Thrombosis Labor And Delivery Superficial Venous Thrombophlebitis Pulmonary Embolism Thromboprophylaxis References During the past century, the frequency of venous thromboembolism (VTE) during the puerperium decreased remarkably as early ambulation became routine practice.

4
00:00:29,387 --> 00:00:37,837
Despite this and other advances in prevention and treatment, thromboembolism remains a leading cause of maternal morbidity and mortality .

5
00:00:37,837 --> 00:00:52,062
Thrombotic pulmonary embolism accounted for almost 10 percent of pregnancy-related deaths in the United States between 2011 and 2015 (Creanga, 2017; Petersen, 2019).

6
00:00:52,062 --> 00:00:59,325
The absolute incidence of VTE during pregnancy is low—1 or 2 cases per 1000 pregnancies.

7
00:00:59,325 --> 00:01:05,487
However, the risk is approximately five times higher than that among women who are not pregnant .

8
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:11,437
Approximately equal numbers of cases are identified antepartum and in the puerperium.

9
00:01:11,437 --> 00:01:18,837
But deep-vein thrombosis alone is more frequent antepartum, and pulmonary embolism is more common postpartum.

10
00:01:18,837 --> 00:01:28,737
During the first 6 weeks of the puerperium, the estimated incidence of a thromboembolic complication is 22 events per 100,000 deliveries.

11
00:01:28,737 --> 00:01:37,962
Although still elevated, the risk falls to approximately 3 cases per 100,000 deliveries during the second 6-week postpartum period .

12
00:01:37,962 --> 00:01:42,412
As many as 2 percent of these women have postthrombotic syndrome .

13
00:01:42,412 --> 00:01:44,650
Pathophysiology.

14
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:55,125
Rudolf Virchow (1856) postulated that stasis, local trauma to the vessel wall, and hypercoagulability predisposed to venous thrombosis.

15
00:01:55,125 --> 00:01:59,000
During pregnancy, the risk for each of these rises.

16
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:08,737
Compression of the pelvic veins and inferior vena cava by the enlarging uterus renders the lower extremity venous system particularly vulnerable to stasis.

17
00:02:08,737 --> 00:02:19,462
Marik and Plante (2008) cite a 50-percent reduction in venous flow velocity in the legs that lasts from the early third trimester until 6 weeks postpartum.

18
00:02:19,462 --> 00:02:25,012
This stasis is the most constant predisposing risk factor for venous thrombosis.

19
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:31,925
Venous stasis, delivery, preeclampsia, and sepsis contribute to endothelial cell injury.

20
00:02:31,925 --> 00:02:42,375
Last, as listed in the Appendix (p. 1228), the synthesis of most clotting factors is markedly enhanced during pregnancy and favors coagulation.

21
00:02:42,375 --> 00:02:48,600
Risk factors for developing VTE during pregnancy are shown in Table 55-1.

22
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:52,887
The most important of these is a personal history of thrombosis.

23
00:02:52,887 --> 00:02:59,925
Specifically, 15 to 25 percent of all VTE cases during pregnancy are recurrent events .

24
00:02:59,925 --> 00:03:05,462
Another important individual risk factor is a genetically determined thrombophilia.

25
00:03:05,462 --> 00:03:16,012
An estimated 20 to 50 percent of women who develop a venous thrombosis during pregnancy or postpartum have an identifiable underlying procoagulant genetic disorder .

26
00:03:16,012 --> 00:03:18,587
Table 55-1.

27
00:03:18,587 --> 00:03:36,500
Some Risk Factors Associated with an Increased Risk for VTE Calculated risks for thromboembolism are approximately doubled in women with multifetal gestation, anemia, hyperemesis, hemorrhage, cesarean delivery, obesity, preeclampsia, and postpartum infection .

28
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:43,200
Scheres and coworkers (2020) had similar observations for women with preeclampsia.

29
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:49,975
Risks were significantly higher among women who had a stillbirth or who underwent peripartum hysterectomy.

30
00:03:49,975 --> 00:03:59,212
Last, as discussed in Chapter 57 (p. 1021), inflammatory bowel disease increases the risk two- to threefold .

31
00:03:59,212 --> 00:04:01,212
Thrombophilias.

32
00:04:01,212 --> 00:04:07,312
Several important regulatory proteins act as inhibitors in the coagulation cascade .

33
00:04:07,312 --> 00:04:14,237
Normal values for many of these proteins during pregnancy are found in the Appendix (p. 1228).

34
00:04:14,237 --> 00:04:21,500
Inherited or acquired deficiencies of these inhibitory proteins are collectively referred to as thrombophilias.

35
00:04:21,500 --> 00:04:26,025
These can lead to hypercoagulability and recurrent VTE .

36
00:04:26,025 --> 00:04:38,737
Although these disorders are collectively present in approximately 15 percent of white European populations, they are responsible for approximately 50 percent of all thromboembolic events during pregnancy .

37
00:04:38,737 --> 00:04:46,700
Some aspects of the more common inherited thrombophilias related to VTE are summarized in Table 55-2.

38
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:49,262
Figure 55-1.

39
00:04:49,262 --> 00:04:54,237
Inherited thrombophilias and their effect(s) on the coagulation cascade.

40
00:04:54,388 --> 00:04:56,975
Table 55-2.

41
00:04:56,925 --> 00:05:06,038
Inherited Thrombophilias and Their Association with Venous Thromboembolism (VTE) in Pregnancy # Inherited Thrombophilias.

42
00:05:06,038 --> 00:05:11,888
Patients with inherited thrombophilic disorders often have a family history of thrombosis.

43
00:05:11,888 --> 00:05:24,425
These mutations are also found in up to half of all patients who present with VTE before the age of 45 years, particularly in those whose event occurred in the absence of well-recognized risk factors.

44
00:05:24,425 --> 00:05:36,900
Of greatest significance is a family history of sudden death due to pulmonary embolism or a history of multiple family members requiring long-term anticoagulation therapy because of recurrent thrombosis .

45
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:48,825
Antithrombin Deficiency Synthesized in the liver, antithrombin is one of the most important inhibitors of thrombin and inactivates thrombin and factor Xa (Rhéaume, 2016).

46
00:05:48,825 --> 00:05:57,088
Notably, the rate of antithrombin interaction with its target is accelerated over 1000–fo1d by heparin.

47
00:05:57,088 --> 00:06:04,263
Antithrombin deficiency may result from hundreds of different mutations that are almost always autosomal dominant.

48
00:06:04,263 --> 00:06:16,363
Type I deficiency results from reduced synthesis of biologically normal antithrombin, and type II deficiency is characterized by normal levels of antithrombin with reduced functional activity.

49
00:06:16,363 --> 00:06:20,138
Homozygous antithrombin deficiency is lethal.

50
00:06:20,138 --> 00:06:25,888
Heterozygous deficiency affects approximately 1 in 2500 individuals.

51
00:06:25,888 --> 00:06:30,188
It is the most thrombogenic of the heritable coagulopathies.

52
00:06:30,188 --> 00:06:34,475
The risk for thrombosis correlates with the degree of deficiency.

53
00:06:34,475 --> 00:06:47,613
Antithrombin deficiency is associated with a 25- to 50fold higher relative risk of VTE in the general population and a sixfold increased risk of thromboembolic complications during pregnancy .

54
00:06:47,613 --> 00:06:54,963
As shown in Table 55-2, those affected have an inordinately high risk of VTE during pregnancy.

55
00:06:54,963 --> 00:07:03,500
Sabadell and associates (2010) studied the outcomes of 18 pregnancies complicated by antithrombin deficiency.

56
00:07:03,500 --> 00:07:10,075
Twelve of these women were treated with therapeutic doses of low-molecular-weight heparin (LMWH).

57
00:07:10,075 --> 00:07:16,400
Three of the untreated patients suffered a thromboembolic episode compared with none in the treated group.

58
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:22,138
Untreated women also had a 50-percent risk of stillbirth and fetal-growth restriction.

59
00:07:22,138 --> 00:07:28,688
By comparison, none of the treated women had a stillbirth, but a fourth developed fetal-growth restriction.

60
00:07:28,688 --> 00:07:40,175
Abbattista and coworkers (2020) reported a 7-percent risk of VTE for women treated with LMWH during pregnancy and a 12-percent risk for those not treated.

61
00:07:40,175 --> 00:07:45,500
Similar results were reported by Ilonczai and colleagues (2015).

62
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:55,538
Last, Garcia-Botella and associates (2016) described a mesenteric vein thrombosis in a pregnant woman with antithrombin deficiency.

63
00:07:55,538 --> 00:08:07,700
Given such risk, affected women are managed during pregnancy with anticoagulation regardless of whether they have had a prior thrombosis (Abbattista, 2020; Bates, 2018).

64
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:20,950
When anticoagulation is necessarily withheld, such as during surgery or delivery, or if VTE develops in an anticoagulated woman, treatment with recombinant human antithrombin may be protective .

65
00:08:20,950 --> 00:08:30,325
Protein C Deficiency When thrombin is bound to thrombomodulin on endothelial cells of small vessels, its procoagulant activities are neutralized.

66
00:08:30,325 --> 00:08:35,638
This binding also activates protein C, a natural anticoagulant.

67
00:08:35,638 --> 00:08:46,700
It, in the presence of protein S, controls thrombin generation, in part, by inactivating factors Va and VIIIa (see Fig. 55-1).

68
00:08:46,700 --> 00:09:02,425
Protein C activity increases modestly but significantly throughout the first half of pregnancy, and some have speculated that this augmentation may play a role in maintaining early pregnancy through both anticoagulant and inflammatory regulatory pathways .

69
00:09:02,425 --> 00:09:09,188
More than 160 different autosomal dominant mutations for the protein C gene have been described .

70
00:09:09,188 --> 00:09:21,125
The prevalence of heterozygous protein C deficiency is 2 to 3 per 1000, but many of these individuals do not have a thrombosis history because the phenotypic expression is highly variable.

71
00:09:21,125 --> 00:09:30,063
These prevalence estimates correspond with functional activity threshold values of 50 to 60 percent, which are used by most laboratories.

72
00:09:30,063 --> 00:09:37,400
As shown in Table 55-2, these are associated with a sixto twelvefold higher risk for VTE .

73
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:38,938
The rare

74
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:45,412
cases of homozygous protein C deficiency may cause fatal neonatal purpura fulminans.

75
00:09:45,362 --> 00:09:52,312
Last, sepsis can result in purpura fulminans in the adult with heterozygous protein C deficiency .

76
00:09:52,312 --> 00:10:05,937
Protein S Deficiency This circulating anticoagulant is activated by protein C, which enhances the capacity of protein S to inactivate factors Va and VIIIa (see Fig. 55-1).

77
00:10:05,937 --> 00:10:18,537
Protein S deficiency may be caused by more than 130 different mutations, with an aggregate heterozygous prevalence of approximately 0.3 to 1.3 per 1000 individuals .

78
00:10:18,537 --> 00:10:25,675
Protein S deficiency may be measured by antigenically determined free, functional, and total S levels.

79
00:10:25,675 --> 00:10:32,025
All three decline substantively during normal gestation (Appendix, p. 1228).

80
00:10:32,025 --> 00:10:39,275
Thus, the diagnosis in pregnant women—as well as in those taking certain oral contraceptives—is difficult.

81
00:10:39,275 --> 00:10:52,812
If screening during pregnancy is necessary, threshold values for free protein S antigen levels in the second and third trimesters have been identified at <30 percent and <24 percent, respectively.

82
00:10:52,812 --> 00:10:56,937
The risk of VTE in pregnancy is increased severalfold .

83
00:10:56,937 --> 00:11:03,550
Among those with a positive family history, the VTE risk in pregnancy is 6 to 7 percent .

84
00:11:03,550 --> 00:11:11,875
Conard and coworkers (1990) described thrombosis in five of 29 pregnant women with protein S deficiency.

85
00:11:11,875 --> 00:11:18,800
They, as well as Burneo and colleagues (2002), reported maternal cerebral vein thrombosis.

86
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:27,025
Neonatal homozygous protein S deficiency is usually associated with purpura fulminans, the fatal clinical phenotype.

87
00:11:27,025 --> 00:11:38,375
Factor V Leiden Mutation This is the most prevalent of the known thrombophilia syndromes and is characterized by resistance of plasma to the anticoagulant effects of activated protein C.

88
00:11:38,375 --> 00:11:45,175
There are several mutations that create this resistance, but the most common is the factor V Leiden mutation.

89
00:11:45,175 --> 00:11:53,837
This missense mutation results from a substitution of glutamine for arginine at position 506 in the factor V polypeptide.

90
00:11:53,837 --> 00:12:04,562
As a result of this mutation, activated factor V is neutralized approximately tenfold more slowly by activated protein C (see Fig. 55-1).

91
00:12:04,562 --> 00:12:07,900
This leads to enhanced thrombin generation.

92
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:13,812
Heterozygous inheritance of factor V Leiden is the most common heritable thrombophilia.

93
00:12:13,812 --> 00:12:27,312
It is found in 3 to 15 percent of select European populations, 1.2 percent of African Americans, and 2.2 of Hispanic Americans, but is virtually absent in African blacks and Asians .

94
00:12:27,312 --> 00:12:35,087
Women who are heterozygous for factor V Leiden account for approximately 40 percent of VTE cases during pregnancy.

95
00:12:35,087 --> 00:12:48,912
However, the actual risk among pregnant women who are heterozygous and who do not have a personal history or a first-degree relative with a thrombotic episode before age 50 years is 5 to 12 events per 1000 births .

96
00:12:48,912 --> 00:12:55,725
In contrast, this risk is at least 10 percent among pregnant women with a personal or family history.

97
00:12:55,725 --> 00:13:06,987
Pregnant women who are homozygous without a personal or family history have a 1to 4-percent risk for VTE, whereas those with such a history have an approximately 17-percent risk .

98
00:13:06,987 --> 00:13:13,187
Diagnosis during pregnancy is performed by DNA analysis for the mutant factor V gene.

99
00:13:13,187 --> 00:13:22,762
Bioassay is not used because of the normal resistance that develops after early pregnancy from alterations in other coagulation protein concentrations.

100
00:13:22,762 --> 00:13:36,275
Of note, activated protein C resistance can also be caused by antiphospholipid syndrome, which is described on page 979 and also detailed in Chapter 62 (p. 1114).

101
00:13:36,275 --> 00:13:50,712
To assess the prognostic significance of maternal factor V Leiden mutation during pregnancy, Kjellberg and associates (2010) compared the outcomes of 491 carriers with those of 1055 controls.

102
00:13:50,712 --> 00:13:55,037
All three of the thromboembolic events occurred among the carriers.

103
00:13:55,037 --> 00:14:02,512
But, preterm birth rates, birthweights, or hypertensive complication rates did not differ between the two groups.

104
00:14:02,512 --> 00:14:14,025
In a prospective observational study of almost 5000 women conducted by the Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units Network, the heterozygous mutant gene incidence was 2.7 percent .

105
00:14:14,025 --> 00:14:23,850
Of three pulmonary emboli and one deep-vein thrombosis cases—a rate of 0.8 per 1000 pregnancies—none was among these carriers.

106
00:14:23,850 --> 00:14:27,900
Moreover, in the heterozygous women, the risks of

107
00:14:28,060 --> 00:14:34,885
preeclampsia, placental abruption, fetalgrowth restriction, or pregnancy loss were not elevated.

108
00:14:34,835 --> 00:14:44,535
The investigators concluded that universal prenatal screening for the Leiden mutation and prophylaxis for carriers without a prior VTE is not indicated.

109
00:14:44,535 --> 00:14:55,635
Prothrombin G20210A Mutation This missense mutation in the prothrombin gene leads to excessive accumulation of prothrombin, which may be converted to thrombin.

110
00:14:55,635 --> 00:15:02,922
Prothrombin levels are increased approximately 30 percent in heterozygotes and 70 percent in homozygotes .

111
00:15:02,922 --> 00:15:12,960
As with factor V Leiden, a personal or family history of VTE in a first-degree relative before age 50 years raises the risk of VTE during pregnancy .

112
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:18,410
For a heterozygous carrier with such a history, the risk exceeds 10 percent.

113
00:15:18,410 --> 00:15:26,347
Without such a history, heterozygous carriers of the mutation have less than a 1percent risk of VTE during pregnancy .

114
00:15:26,347 --> 00:15:36,297
Silver and coworkers (2010) tested nearly 4200 women for the prothrombin G20210A mutation.

115
00:15:36,297 --> 00:15:44,935
A total of 157—or 3.8 percent—of the women carried the mutation, and only one of these was homozygous.

116
00:15:44,935 --> 00:15:54,060
Carriers had similar rates of pregnancy loss, preeclampsia, fetal-growth restriction, and placental abruption compared with noncarriers.

117
00:15:54,060 --> 00:15:59,385
Three thromboembolic events occurred in women who tested negative for the mutation.

118
00:15:59,385 --> 00:16:12,460
Homozygous patients, or those who are doubly heterozygous for a G20210A mutation with a factor V Leiden mutation, have a greater thromboembolism risk than heterozygous carriers .

119
00:16:12,460 --> 00:16:23,447
Others have provided detailed information on pregnancy outcomes in women with such rare compound thrombophilias (Carroll, 2018; Lim, 2016).

120
00:16:23,447 --> 00:16:37,060
Hyperhomocysteinemia The most common cause of elevated homocysteine is the C667T thermolabile mutation of the 5, 10– methylene–tetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) enzyme.

121
00:16:37,060 --> 00:16:40,147
Inheritance is autosomal recessive.

122
00:16:40,147 --> 00:16:53,985
Elevated homocysteine levels may also result from deficiency of one of several enzymes involved in methionine metabolism and from correctible nutritional deficiencies of folic acid, vitamin B6, or vitamin B12.

123
00:16:53,985 --> 00:17:01,147
During normal pregnancy, mean homocysteine plasma concentrations decline and make a diagnosis difficult .

124
00:17:01,147 --> 00:17:19,572
A metaanalysis by den Heijer and associates (2005) reported an association between MTHFR polymorphisms and a slightly greater risk for thrombosis in international studies, but no such association was found in North American studies (den Heijer, 2005).

125
00:17:19,572 --> 00:17:25,072
The authors speculated that folic acid supplementation could explain the difference.

126
00:17:25,072 --> 00:17:32,060
Recall that folic acid serves as a cofactor in the remethylation reaction of homocysteine to methionine.

127
00:17:32,060 --> 00:17:48,972
Similarly, the American College of Chest Physicians posited that the lack of an association with thromboembolism could reflect the physiological reductions in homocysteine levels associated with pregnancy as well as the effects of widespread prenatal folic acid supplementation .

128
00:17:48,972 --> 00:18:04,772
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) has concluded that there is insufficient evidence to support assessment of MTHFR polymorphisms or measurement of fasting homocysteine levels in the evaluation for VTE.

129
00:18:04,772 --> 00:18:13,685
Other Thrombophilia Mutations Protein Z is a vitamin K–dependent protein that serves as a cofactor in factor Xa inactivation.

130
00:18:13,685 --> 00:18:25,585
Studies have found that low protein Z levels are associated with an elevated thromboembolism risk in nonpregnant patients and may be implicated in the pathogenesis of poor pregnancy outcomes .

131
00:18:25,585 --> 00:18:33,572
Similarly, plasminogen activator inhibitor type 1 (PAI-I) is an important regulator of fibrinolysis.

132
00:18:33,572 --> 00:18:39,922
Certain polymorphisms in the gene promoter have been associated with slightly higher VTE risks.

133
00:18:39,922 --> 00:18:55,397
Although these thrombophilias may exacerbate risk among patients when coinherited with other thrombophilias, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) has concluded that evidence to recommend screening is insufficient.

134
00:18:55,397 --> 00:18:58,385
# Acquired Thrombophilias.

135
00:18:58,385 --> 00:19:08,360
Some examples of acquired hypercoagulable states include antiphospholipid syndrome (APS), heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, and cancer.

136
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:14,160
Antiphospholipid Syndrome This prothrombotic disorder can affect both the venous and

137
00:19:14,308 --> 00:19:16,820
arterial circulations.

138
00:19:16,770 --> 00:19:30,170
The deeper veins of the lower limbs and the cerebral arterial circulation are the most frequent sites of venous and arterial thrombosis, respectively (Garcia, 2018; Konkle, 2018).

139
00:19:30,170 --> 00:19:40,383
Besides thrombosis, the other major clinical manifestations of APS are adverse obstetrical outcomes (Table 11-4, p. 205).

140
00:19:40,383 --> 00:20:03,083
Criteria for APS include (1) at least one otherwise unexplained fetal death at or beyond 10 weeks; (2) at least one preterm birth before 34 weeks’ gestation because of eclampsia, severe preeclampsia, or placental insufficiency; or (3) at least three unexplained consecutive spontaneous abortions before 10 weeks.

141
00:20:03,083 --> 00:20:08,683
The syndrome is discussed in detail in Chapter 62 (p. 1114).

142
00:20:08,683 --> 00:20:21,445
Testing for antiphospholipid antibodies should be performed in women with an unexplained arterial or venous thromboembolism or a history of one fetal loss or three or more recurrent embryonic or fetal losses .

143
00:20:21,445 --> 00:20:29,258
At this time, preterm severe preeclampsia and early-onset placental insufficiency do not support such testing.

144
00:20:29,258 --> 00:20:40,795
That said, there is evidence that antiphospholipid positivity confers a worse prognosis for pregnancies complicated by HELLP syndrome (Pécourt, 2021).

145
00:20:40,795 --> 00:20:58,920
These patients should be tested for the presence of three factors: (1) lupus anticoagulant, (2) anticardiolipin immunoglobulin G and M (IgG and IgM) antibodies, and (3) anti-β2 glycoprotein-I IgG and IgM antibodies.

146
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:06,083
If any of these laboratory test results are positive, a confirmatory test is performed 12 weeks later .

147
00:21:06,083 --> 00:21:20,945
Based on their study of 750 singleton pregnancies complicated by APS, Saccone and colleagues (2017) found that anticardiolipin antibody is the most common sole antiphospholipid antibody present.

148
00:21:20,945 --> 00:21:27,845
Women with APS have a 5- to 12-percent risk of thrombosis during pregnancy or the puerperium.

149
00:21:27,845 --> 00:21:34,545
Indeed, up to 25 percent of these thrombotic events occur during pregnancy or in the puerperium.

150
00:21:34,545 --> 00:21:38,583
# Thrombophilias and Pregnancy Complications.

151
00:21:38,583 --> 00:21:46,820
Contrary to past teaching, no definite causal link between inherited thrombophilias and adverse pregnancy outcomes exists .

152
00:21:46,820 --> 00:21:54,220
The confusion was caused by a number of observational studies that were later followed by large prospective cohort studies.

153
00:21:54,220 --> 00:22:11,645
Currently, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) does not recommend inherited thrombophilia screening for women with a history of fetal loss or adverse pregnancy outcomes such as fetal-growth restriction, placental abruption, and preeclampsia.

154
00:22:11,645 --> 00:22:16,820
There are no convincing data that link inherited thrombophilias and fetal loss.

155
00:22:16,820 --> 00:22:25,958
It follows that there is no benefit to aspirin or heparin therapy as might accrue for recurrent pregnancy loss due to antiphospholipid antibodies .

156
00:22:25,958 --> 00:22:34,833
Stillbirths also are not more common with heterozygous mutations, but there is a weak association with homozygous factor V Leiden mutation .

157
00:22:34,833 --> 00:22:41,670
Last, there is insufficient evidence to screen for inherited thrombophilias in women with a fetal death .

158
00:22:41,670 --> 00:22:53,783
Fetal-growth restriction also is not more common with inherited thrombophilias (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Infante-Rivard, 2002).

159
00:22:53,783 --> 00:23:06,670
Likewise, there have been a number of prospective cohort studies that showed no association of inherited thrombophilias with placental abruption (Dizon-Townson, 2005; Silver, 2010).

160
00:23:06,670 --> 00:23:22,608
Conversely, Alfirevic and coworkers (2002) reported an association with these and both heterozygosity and homozygosity for factor V Leiden mutations as well as heterozygous prothrombin G20210A mutation.

161
00:23:22,608 --> 00:23:29,195
Studies showing an association of inherited thrombophilias and preeclampsia also are conflicting .

162
00:23:29,195 --> 00:23:40,445
One large systematic review and metaanalysis of nearly 22,000 women did not show an association between factor V Leiden or prothrombin gene mutations with preeclampsia .

163
00:23:40,445 --> 00:23:49,158
Similarly, a more recent prospective study failed to show an association between preeclampsia and 12 hereditary thrombophilias .

164
00:23:49,158 --> 00:24:02,308
Thus, because of uncertainties in the magnitude of risk and in the benefits of prophylaxis given to prevent pregnancy complications in women with heritable thrombophilias, it remains unproven that universal screening is

165
00:24:02,476 --> 00:24:04,138
indicated .

166
00:24:04,088 --> 00:24:15,388
In contrast, the association between APS and adverse pregnancy outcomes—including fetal loss, recurrent pregnancy loss, and preeclampsia—is much stronger .

167
00:24:15,388 --> 00:24:18,213
# Thrombophilia Screening.

168
00:24:18,213 --> 00:24:28,388
Given the relatively high incidence of thrombophilia in the population and the low incidence of VTE, universal screening during pregnancy is not costeffective.

169
00:24:28,388 --> 00:24:32,363
Thus, a selective screening strategy is required.

170
00:24:32,363 --> 00:24:57,988
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) recommends that thrombophilia screening be considered in the following clinical circumstances: (1) a personal history of VTE with or without a recurrent risk factor such as fractures, surgery, or prolonged immobilization; and (2) a firstdegree relative (parent or sibling) with a history of a high-risk inherited thrombophilia.

171
00:24:57,988 --> 00:25:08,301
As discussed previously, testing for inherited thrombophilias in women who have experienced recurrent fetal loss or adverse pregnancy outcomes is not recommended.

172
00:25:08,301 --> 00:25:15,113
Methods of screening for the more common inherited thrombophilias are shown in Table 55-3.

173
00:25:15,113 --> 00:25:27,476
Whenever possible, laboratory testing is performed at least 6 weeks after the thrombotic event, while the patient is not pregnant, and when she is not receiving anticoagulation or hormonal therapy.

174
00:25:27,476 --> 00:25:32,276
Last, screening for hyperhomocysteinemia is not recommended .

175
00:25:32,276 --> 00:25:34,938
Table 55-3.

176
00:25:34,938 --> 00:25:38,963
How to Test for Thrombophilias Deep-Vein Thrombosis.

177
00:25:38,963 --> 00:25:41,788
# Clinical Presentation.

178
00:25:41,788 --> 00:25:48,176
During pregnancy, most venous thromboses are confined to the deep veins of the lower extremity.

179
00:25:48,176 --> 00:25:54,551
Most of these are located in the iliofemoral and iliac veins without involvement of the calf veins .

180
00:25:54,551 --> 00:26:01,913
In contrast, in the general population, more than 80 percent of deep-vein thromboses involve calf veins .

181
00:26:01,913 --> 00:26:04,838
The vast majority are left sided .

182
00:26:04,838 --> 00:26:16,763
Greer (2003) hypothesizes that this results from compression of the left iliac vein by the right iliac and ovarian arteries, both of which cross the vein only on the left side.

183
00:26:16,763 --> 00:26:24,501
Yet, as described in Chapter 56 (p. 994), the ureter is compressed more on the right side.

184
00:26:24,501 --> 00:26:31,138
Signs and symptoms vary depending on the degree of occlusion and the intensity of the inflammatory response.

185
00:26:31,138 --> 00:26:39,188
Classically, thrombosis involving the lower extremity is abrupt in onset, and there is pain and edema of the leg and thigh.

186
00:26:39,188 --> 00:26:44,651
The thrombus typically involves much of the deep-venous system to the iliofemoral region.

187
00:26:44,651 --> 00:26:51,663
Occasionally, reflex arterial spasm causes a pale, cool extremity with diminished pulsations.

188
00:26:51,663 --> 00:26:55,688
Calf measurements may identify a discordance in size.

189
00:26:55,688 --> 00:27:01,526
Alternatively, there may be appreciable clot, yet little pain, heat, or swelling.

190
00:27:01,526 --> 00:27:12,126
Importantly, calf pain, either spontaneous or in response to squeezing or to Achilles tendon stretching—Homans sign—may be caused by a strained muscle or contusion.

191
00:27:12,126 --> 00:27:24,326
Importantly, 30 to 60 percent of women with a confirmed lower-extremity acute deep-vein thrombosis (DVT) have an asymptomatic pulmonary embolism (p. 986).

192
00:27:24,326 --> 00:27:26,726
# Diagnosis.

193
00:27:26,726 --> 00:27:33,813
Clinical diagnosis of DVT is difficult without testing and is confirmed in only 10 percent of cases .

194
00:27:33,813 --> 00:27:43,888
Another challenge is that many of the common diagnostic tests that have been investigated extensively in nonpregnant patients have not been validated appropriately in pregnancy .

195
00:27:43,888 --> 00:27:51,426
Shown in Figure 55-2 is one diagnostic algorithm that can be used for evaluation of pregnant women.

196
00:27:51,426 --> 00:27:57,163
With a few modifications, we follow a similar evaluation at Parkland Hospital.

197
00:27:57,163 --> 00:27:59,726
Figure 55-2.

198
00:27:59,726 --> 00:28:07,188
Algorithm to evalate suspected venous thromboembolism in pregnancy depending upon number of YEARS criteria met.

199
00:28:07,188 --> 00:28:11,213
Evidence of DVT should prompt compression ultrasound.

200
00:28:11,213 --> 00:28:19,251
aThe three YEARS criteria are clinical signs of DVT, hemoptysis, and PE as the most likely diagnosis.

201
00:28:19,251 --> 00:28:30,901
bThe American Thoracic Society and Society of Thoracic Radiology recommend CXR; cThe American Society of Hematology recommends pulmonary V/Q scanning.

202
00:28:30,901 --> 00:28:50,413
CT = computed tomography; CTA = computed tomography angiography; CXR = chest x-ray; DVT = deep-vein thrombosis; MR = magnetic resonance; PE = pulmonary embolism; V/Q scan = ventilation perfusion scan.

203
00:28:50,413 --> 00:29:01,488
From Bates, 2018; Guyatt, 2012; Leung, 2011; van der Hulle, 2019; van der Pol, 2019.

204
00:29:01,488 --> 00:29:03,163
Compression

205
00:29:03,316 --> 00:29:12,378
Ultrasonography With suspected DVT, the initial diagnostic test recommended is compression ultrasonography of the proximal leg veins .

206
00:29:12,328 --> 00:29:18,891
The diagnosis is based on the noncompressibility and typical echoarchitecture of a thrombosed vein.

207
00:29:18,891 --> 00:29:30,128
For nonpregnant patients with suspected thrombosis, the safety of withholding anticoagulation for 1 week has been established in the setting of a normal compression ultrasound examination .

208
00:29:30,128 --> 00:29:43,953
Serial compression examinations are then performed because isolated undetected calf thromboses that ultimately extend into the proximal veins will do so within 1 to 2 weeks of presentation in approximately a fourth of patients.

209
00:29:43,953 --> 00:29:51,516
In pregnant women, however, normal findings with ultrasonography do not always exclude a pulmonary embolism.

210
00:29:51,516 --> 00:30:01,916
This is because the thrombosis may have already embolized or because it arose from iliac or other deep-pelvic veins, which are less accessible to ultrasound evaluation.

211
00:30:01,916 --> 00:30:10,128
Two studies are helpful for evaluating whether serial examinations are necessary for pregnant women suspected of having a DVT.

212
00:30:10,128 --> 00:30:18,978
Chan and associates (2013) studied 221 pregnant and postpartum women presenting with a suspected DVT.

213
00:30:18,978 --> 00:30:26,616
The 205 women with a negative initial study result underwent serial testing, which was negative in all cases.

214
00:30:26,616 --> 00:30:31,466
Of these, one woman had a pulmonary embolism 7 weeks later.

215
00:30:31,466 --> 00:30:39,353
Le Gal and colleagues (2012) studied 210 pregnant and postpartum women with a suspected DVT.

216
00:30:39,353 --> 00:30:47,353
Of these, 177 women without a DVT were not anticoagulated and did not undergo serial testing.

217
00:30:47,353 --> 00:30:52,341
Two had an objectively confirmed thrombosis diagnosed within 3 months.

218
00:30:52,341 --> 00:31:02,628
In sum, these data suggest that a negative single complete compression ultrasonography study may safely exclude the diagnosis of DVT in most pregnant women.

219
00:31:02,628 --> 00:31:10,728
Magnetic Resonance Imaging This imaging technique allows excellent delineation of anatomical detail above the inguinal ligament.

220
00:31:10,728 --> 00:31:20,278
Thus, in many cases, magnetic resonance (MR) imaging is immensely useful for diagnosis of iliofemoral and pelvic vein thrombosis.

221
00:31:20,278 --> 00:31:24,941
The venous system can also be reconstructed using MR venography .

222
00:31:24,941 --> 00:31:34,153
MR imaging is 100-percent sensitive and 90percent specific for detection of venographically proven DVT in nonpregnant patients .

223
00:31:34,153 --> 00:31:46,303
Importantly, almost half of those without DVT were found to have nonthrombotic conditions that included cellulitis, myositis, edema, hematomas, and superficial phlebitis.

224
00:31:46,303 --> 00:31:57,328
D-dimer Screening Tests These specific fibrin degradation products are generated when fibrinolysin (plasminogen) degrades fibrin, as occurs in thromboembolism.

225
00:31:57,328 --> 00:32:04,303
Their measurement is usually incorporated into diagnostic algorithms for VTE in nonpregnant patients .

226
00:32:04,303 --> 00:32:10,503
This method was shown to be accurate in the YEARS study (van der Hulle, 2017).

227
00:32:10,503 --> 00:32:16,716
Screening with the D-dimer test in pregnancy, however, is problematic for several reasons.

228
00:32:16,716 --> 00:32:29,041
Depending on assay sensitivity, D-dimer serum levels rise with gestational age along with substantively elevated plasma fibrinogen concentrations (Appendix, p. 1228) .

229
00:32:29,041 --> 00:32:37,391
Levels are also affected by multifetal gestation and cesarean delivery (Hu, 2020; Miyamoto, 2020).

230
00:32:37,391 --> 00:32:46,966
Last, D-dimer concentrations can also be elevated in pregnancy complications such as placental abruption, preeclampsia, and sepsis .

231
00:32:46,966 --> 00:32:53,791
Three large prospective studies have evaluated D-dimer testing in pregnant women to diagnose VTE.

232
00:32:53,791 --> 00:33:04,578
Righini and colleagues (2018) found that D-dimer measurement, bilateral leg ultrasound, and computed tomographyangiogram safely ruled out VTE.

233
00:33:04,578 --> 00:33:14,703
Subsequently, van der Pol and coworkers (2019) used a pregnancy-adapted YEARS algorithm to diagnose suspected pulmonary embolism.

234
00:33:14,703 --> 00:33:20,478
The algorithm used D-dimer measurements and clinical suspicion to confirm VTE.

235
00:33:20,478 --> 00:33:39,153
With none of the clinical criteria present, and with the D-dimer level <1000 ng/mL, or with one or more clinical criteria present and with the D-dimer level <500 ng/mL, pulmonary embolism was safely excluded (see Fig. 55-2).

236
00:33:39,153 --> 00:33:48,703
Last, the DiPEP study showed that neither clinical risk factors nor D-dimer levels were accurate in identifying pulmonary embolism in pregnant women .

237
00:33:48,703 --> 00:33:52,716
They concluded that pregnant women with suspected pulmonary

238
00:33:52,876 --> 00:33:56,326
embolism should all undergo imaging studies.

239
00:33:56,276 --> 00:34:01,176
The findings of the YEARS study and subsequent others will no doubt guide care.

240
00:34:01,176 --> 00:34:12,938
However, currently the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends against the use of D-dimer assays to diagnose VTE in pregnant women.

241
00:34:12,938 --> 00:34:21,613
We agree, and in our view, these positive results need to be duplicated before adaptation of their widespread use in pregnant women.

242
00:34:21,613 --> 00:34:28,776
Still, a negative D-dimer assay should be reassuring, unless VTE is the most likely diagnosis .

243
00:34:28,776 --> 00:34:32,388
# Management of Venous Thromboembolism.

244
00:34:32,388 --> 00:34:40,388
Optimal management of VTE during pregnancy has not undergone major clinical study to provide evidence-based practices.

245
00:34:40,388 --> 00:34:46,738
There is, however, consensus for treatment with anticoagulation and limited activity.

246
00:34:46,738 --> 00:34:50,238
Thrombophilia testing does not change treatment .

247
00:34:50,238 --> 00:34:57,538
Anticoagulation is initiated with either unfractionated heparin (UFH) or LMWH .

248
00:34:57,538 --> 00:35:09,576
Although either type is acceptable, most recommend one of the LMWHs (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020b; Kearon, 2016).

249
00:35:09,576 --> 00:35:27,238
The American Society of Hematology recommends preferential use of LMWH during pregnancy because of better bioavailability, longer plasma half-life, more predictable dose response, reduced risks of osteoporosis and thrombocytopenia, and less frequent dosing .

250
00:35:27,238 --> 00:35:29,913
Table 55-4.

251
00:35:29,913 --> 00:35:41,076
Anticoagulation Regimen Definitions The duration of full anticoagulation varies, and no studies have defined the optimal duration for pregnancy-related thromboembolism.

252
00:35:41,076 --> 00:35:46,788
In nonpregnant patients, evidence supports a minimum treatment duration of 3 months .

253
00:35:46,788 --> 00:36:03,313
For pregnant patients, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends therapeutic anticoagulation for 3 to 6 months followed by intermediate or prophylactic therapy for the duration of pregnancy and the postpartum period.

254
00:36:03,313 --> 00:36:10,338
If the woman is not breastfeeding, direct acting oral agents can be considered (p. 985).

255
00:36:10,338 --> 00:36:21,938
Recall that pulmonary embolism develops in as many as 60 percent of patients with untreated venous thrombosis, and anticoagulation decreases this risk to less than 5 percent.

256
00:36:21,938 --> 00:36:32,526
In nonpregnant patients, the mortality rate with a pulmonary embolism approximates 1 percent (Douketis, 1998; Pollack, 2011).

257
00:36:32,526 --> 00:36:37,101
With DVT, over several days, leg pain dissipates.

258
00:36:37,101 --> 00:36:41,351
After symptoms have abated, graded ambulation is begun.

259
00:36:41,351 --> 00:36:46,288
Elastic stockings are fitted, and anticoagulation is continued.

260
00:36:46,288 --> 00:36:50,476
Recovery to this stage usually takes 7 to 10 days.

261
00:36:50,476 --> 00:36:58,713
Graduated compression stockings can be continued for 2 years after the diagnosis to reduce the incidence of postthrombotic syndrome .

262
00:36:58,713 --> 00:37:06,238
Symptoms include chronic leg paresthesias or pain, intractable edema, skin changes, and leg ulcers.

263
00:37:06,238 --> 00:37:14,701
According to the American Society of Hematology, catheter-directed thrombolytic therapy does not mitigate against this complication .

264
00:37:14,701 --> 00:37:25,913
Unfractionated Heparin This agent should be considered for the initial treatment of thromboembolism and in situations in which delivery, surgery, or thrombolysis may be necessary .

265
00:37:25,913 --> 00:37:49,763
UFH can be administered by one of two alternatives: (1) initial intravenous therapy followed by adjusted-dose subcutaneous UFH given every 12 hours; or (2) twice-daily, adjusted-dose subcutaneous UFH with doses adjusted to prolong the activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) into the therapeutic range 6 hours post injection .

266
00:37:49,763 --> 00:37:52,513
Table 55-5.

267
00:37:52,513 --> 00:38:00,526
Some Recommendations for Thromboprophylaxis During Pregnancy For intravenous therapy, several protocols are acceptable.

268
00:38:00,526 --> 00:38:10,888
In general, if UFH is used, it is initiated with a bolus intravenous dose of 70 to 100 U/kg, which is 5000 to 10,000 U.

269
00:38:10,888 --> 00:38:20,126
This is followed by continuous intravenous infusions beginning at 1000 U/hr or 15 to 20 U/kg/hr.

270
00:38:20,126 --> 00:38:27,638
This infusion rate is titrated to achieve an aPTT 1.5 to 2.5 times control values .

271
00:38:27,638 --> 00:38:42,401
Intravenous anticoagulation is maintained for about 5 to 7 days, after which treatment is converted to subcutaneous heparin to maintain the aPTT to at least 1.5 to 2.5 times control throughout the dosing interval.

272
00:38:42,401 --> 00:38:52,876
For women with lupus anticoagulant, aPTT does not accurately assess heparin anticoagulation, and thus anti–factor Xa levels are preferred.

273
00:38:53,044 --> 00:39:06,694
Low-molecular-weight Heparin This is a family of derivatives of unfractionated heparin, and their molecular weights average 4000 to 5000 daltons compared with 12,000 to 16,000 daltons for conventional heparin.

274
00:39:06,644 --> 00:39:14,419
None of these heparins cross the placenta, and all exert their anticoagulant activity by activating antithrombin.

275
00:39:14,419 --> 00:39:25,206
UFH has equivalent activity against factor Xa and thrombin compared with LMWHs, which have greater activity against factor Xa than against thrombin.

276
00:39:25,206 --> 00:39:45,494
They also have a more predictable anticoagulant response and fewer bleeding complications than UFH because of their better bioavailability, longer halflife, dose-independent clearance, and decreased interference with platelets (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020b; Bates, 2018).

277
00:39:45,494 --> 00:39:50,719
Their longer half-life prohibits neuraxial analgesia in laboring women.

278
00:39:50,719 --> 00:39:57,769
LMWH compounds are cleared by the kidneys and must be used cautiously when there is renal dysfunction.

279
00:39:57,769 --> 00:40:07,144
Several studies have shown that VTE is treated effectively with LMWH (Quinlan, 2004; Tapson, 2008).

280
00:40:07,144 --> 00:40:21,144
Using serial venograms, Breddin and associates (2001) observed that these compounds were more effective than UFH in reducing thrombus size without increasing mortality rates or major bleeding complications.

281
00:40:21,144 --> 00:40:29,469
Several different treatment regimens using LMWH for treatment of acute VTE are listed in Table 55-4 .

282
00:40:29,469 --> 00:40:35,544
Whether LMWH requires adjustments during the course of pregnancy is controversial .

283
00:40:35,544 --> 00:40:48,244
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends monitoring with an anti-Xa level 4 hours after an injection with dose adjustment to maintain a therapeutic level.

284
00:40:48,244 --> 00:40:58,781
Large studies using clinical end points that demonstrate an optimal therapeutic range or show that dose adjustments increase therapy, safety, or efficacy are lacking.

285
00:40:58,781 --> 00:41:11,819
Accordingly, the American Society of Hematology and others have concluded that routine monitoring with anti-Xa levels is difficult to justify (Bates, 2018; McDonnell, 2017).

286
00:41:11,819 --> 00:41:18,769
In general, heparin pharmacokinetics includes more rapid clearance of heparins as pregnancy progresses.

287
00:41:18,769 --> 00:41:26,969
The peaks of anti-Xa activity of enoxaparin (Lovenox) across pregnancy are shown in Figure 55-3.

288
00:41:26,969 --> 00:41:41,744
Similarly, tinzaparin (Innohep) with a dosage of 75 to 175 U/kg/d was necessary to achieve peak anti–factor Xa levels of 0.1 to 1.0 U/mL .

289
00:41:41,744 --> 00:41:59,994
In studies of dalteparin (Fragmin) pharmacokinetics, conventional starting doses of dalteparin—100 U/kg every 12 hours—were likely insufficient to maintain full anticoagulation in pregnant women (Barbour, 2004; Jacobsen, 2003).

290
00:41:59,994 --> 00:42:06,019
Thus, slightly higher doses than that shown in Table 55-4 may be required.

291
00:42:06,019 --> 00:42:08,706
Figure 55-3.

292
00:42:08,706 --> 00:42:19,319
Anti–factor Xa activity with repeated daily administration of 40 mg enoxaparin subcutaneously in nonpregnant women and those from each trimester .

293
00:42:19,319 --> 00:42:30,681
Any concerns regarding teratogenicity of enoxaparin have been obviated (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020b; Bates, 2021).

294
00:42:30,681 --> 00:42:43,019
A comprehensive study of 1267 pregnant women treated with tinzaparin found no cases of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, maternal deaths, or complications from regional analgesia .

295
00:42:43,019 --> 00:42:49,494
A total of 43 women (3.4 percent) required medical intervention for bleeding.

296
00:42:49,494 --> 00:42:55,519
Of 15 stillbirths, four were judged as possibly being related to tinzaparin use.

297
00:42:55,519 --> 00:42:59,931
LMWHs have been judged to be safe during breastfeeding .

298
00:42:59,931 --> 00:43:13,344
Labor and Delivery Women receiving either therapeutic or prophylactic anticoagulation should be converted from LMWH to the shorter-half-life UFH in the last month of pregnancy or sooner if delivery appears imminent.

299
00:43:13,344 --> 00:43:25,169
The purpose of conversion to UFH has less to do with any risk of maternal bleeding at the time of delivery, but rather with neuraxial blockade complicated by an epidural or spinal hematoma .

300
00:43:25,169 --> 00:43:43,756
The Society for Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology recommends that UFH low-dose thromboprophylaxis be withheld for 4 to 6 hours; UFH intermediate-dose thromboprophylaxis for 12 hours; and UFH therapeutic dose for 24 hours before neuraxial analgesia .

301
00:43:43,756 --> 00:43:52,794
For LMWH, the Society recommends to withhold low doses for 12 hours; intermediate doses 12 to 24 hours;

302
00:43:52,948 --> 00:43:56,285
and therapeutic doses 24 hours.

303
00:43:56,235 --> 00:44:02,698
If a woman begins labor while taking UFH, clearance can be verified by an aPTT.

304
00:44:02,698 --> 00:44:15,460
Reversal of heparin with protamine sulfate—1 mg per 100 units heparin with a maximum dose of 50 mg—is rarely required and is not indicated with a prophylactic dose of heparin.

305
00:44:15,460 --> 00:44:23,410
For women in whom anticoagulation therapy has temporarily been discontinued, pneumatic compression devices are recommended.

306
00:44:23,410 --> 00:44:33,773
Warfarin Compounds Vitamin K antagonists are generally contraindicated because they readily cross the placenta and cause fetal death and malformations from hemorrhages.

307
00:44:33,773 --> 00:44:38,773
This is discussed in detail in Chapter 8 (p. 156).

308
00:44:38,773 --> 00:44:43,348
Their sole antepartum use is in women with a mechanical heart valve .

309
00:44:43,348 --> 00:44:48,798
These compounds do not accumulate in breast milk and are thus safe during breastfeeding.

310
00:44:48,798 --> 00:44:56,535
Postpartum venous thrombosis is usually treated with intravenous heparin and oral warfarin initiated simultaneously.

311
00:44:56,535 --> 00:45:01,785
The initial dose of warfarin is 5 to 10 mg for the first 2 days.

312
00:45:01,785 --> 00:45:08,985
Subsequent doses are titrated to achieve an international normalized ratio (INR) of 2 to 3.

313
00:45:08,985 --> 00:45:25,810
To avoid paradoxical thrombosis and skin necrosis from the early antiprotein C effect of warfarin, these women are maintained on therapeutic doses of UFH or LMWH for 5 days and until the INR is in a therapeutic range for 2 consecutive days .

314
00:45:25,810 --> 00:45:31,035
Treatment in the puerperium may require larger doses of anticoagulant.

315
00:45:31,035 --> 00:45:39,423
Brooks and colleagues (2002) compared anticoagulation in postpartum women with that of age-matched nonpregnant controls.

316
00:45:39,423 --> 00:45:51,910
The former required a significantly larger median total dose of warfarin—45 versus 24 mg—and a longer time—7 versus 4 days—to achieve the target INR.

317
00:45:51,910 --> 00:46:05,348
Direct-acting Oral Anticoagulants Of these newer oral anticoagulants, dabigatran (Pradaxa) inhibits thrombin and rivaroxaban (Xarelto) and apixaban (Eliquis) inhibit factor Xa.

318
00:46:05,348 --> 00:46:11,260
These agents are becoming the drugs of choice for nonpregnant subjects with thromboembolism .

319
00:46:11,260 --> 00:46:25,123
Currently, very few reports address these newer agents during pregnancy and breastfeeding, and thus the human reproductive risks are essentially unknown (Lameijer, 2018; Rosenbloom, 2019).

320
00:46:25,123 --> 00:46:33,660
Despite this, the GARFIELD-VTE study reported that direct-acting oral anticoagulant use in pregnant women was gaining favor .

321
00:46:33,660 --> 00:46:41,110
Dabigatran crosses the placenta, however, it is unknown whether any of these agents are excreted in breast milk .

322
00:46:41,110 --> 00:46:56,498
At this time, because of the potential for infant harm, a decision should be made to either avoid breastfeeding or use an alternative anticoagulant, such as warfarin, in breastfeeding women (Bates, 2021; Kruger, 2019).

323
00:46:56,498 --> 00:47:00,198
# Complications of Anticoagulation.

324
00:47:00,198 --> 00:47:08,535
Three significant complications associated with anticoagulation are hemorrhage, thrombocytopenia, and osteoporosis.

325
00:47:08,535 --> 00:47:14,585
The latter two are unique to heparin, and their risk may be reduced with LMWHs.

326
00:47:14,585 --> 00:47:21,735
The most serious complication is hemorrhage, which is more likely if there has been recent surgery or lacerations.

327
00:47:21,735 --> 00:47:26,635
Troublesome bleeding is also more likely if the heparin dosage is excessive.

328
00:47:26,635 --> 00:47:38,773
Unfortunately, management schemes using laboratory testing to identify when a heparin dosage is sufficient to inhibit further thrombosis, yet not cause serious hemorrhage, have been discouraging.

329
00:47:38,773 --> 00:47:53,460
Heparin-induced Thrombocytopenia There are two types—the most common is a nonimmune, benign, reversible thrombocytopenia that develops within the first few days of therapy and resolves in approximately 5 days without therapy cessation.

330
00:47:53,460 --> 00:48:05,773
The second is the severe form of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, which results from an immune reaction involving IgG antibodies directed against complexes of platelet factor 4 and heparin.

331
00:48:05,773 --> 00:48:21,348
The diagnosis of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is based on a drop in the platelet count of more than 50 percent or thrombosis beginning 5 to 10 days after the start of heparin in association with the appearance of platelet-activating HIT antibodies.

332
00:48:21,348 --> 00:48:31,410
The fall in platelet count in HIT occurs rapidly—over a period of 1 to 3 days— and is assessed relative to the highest platelet count after the start of heparin.

333
00:48:31,410 --> 00:48:35,323
The typical nadir is 40,000 to 80,000

334
00:48:35,476 --> 00:48:37,838
platelets per microliter .

335
00:48:37,788 --> 00:48:47,838
Although the incidence of HIT is approximately 3 to 5 percent in nonpregnant individuals, it is <0.1 percent in obstetrical patients .

336
00:48:47,838 --> 00:48:58,938
Fausett and coworkers (2001) reported no cases among 244 heparin-treated gravidas compared with 10 among 244 nonpregnant patients.

337
00:48:58,938 --> 00:49:08,388
Accordingly, the American College of Chest Physicians recommends against platelet count monitoring when the risk of HIT is considered to be <1 percent .

338
00:49:08,388 --> 00:49:14,426
In others, they suggest monitoring every 2 or 3 days from day 4 until 14.

339
00:49:14,426 --> 00:49:22,338
Women with a presumptive diagnosis of HIT should have laboratory testing for antiplatelet antibodies or by functional assays.

340
00:49:22,338 --> 00:49:31,063
However, these tests often require several days to return results, so initial management should be directed by clinical findings.

341
00:49:31,063 --> 00:49:36,301
Heparin therapy is stopped, and alternative anticoagulation initiated.

342
00:49:36,301 --> 00:49:42,801
LMWH may not be entirely safe because it has some cross reactivity with UFH.

343
00:49:42,801 --> 00:49:51,576
The American Society of Hematology recommends use of fondaparinux (Arixtra), which is a pentasaccharide factor Xa inhibitor .

344
00:49:51,576 --> 00:49:59,301
Successful use in pregnancy has been reported (De Carolis, 2015; Elsaigh, 2015).

345
00:49:59,301 --> 00:50:02,826
Last, platelet transfusions are avoided .

346
00:50:02,826 --> 00:50:13,413
Heparin-induced Osteoporosis Bone loss may develop with long-term heparin administration—usually 6 months or longer—and is more prevalent in cigarette smokers.

347
00:50:13,413 --> 00:50:30,813
UFH can cause osteopenia, and this is less likely with LMWHs. Women treated with any heparin should be encouraged to take an oral daily 1500-mg calcium supplement (Cunningham, 2005; Lockwood, 2012).

348
00:50:30,813 --> 00:50:34,338
# Anticoagulation and Abortion.

349
00:50:34,338 --> 00:50:40,688
The treatment of DVT with heparin does not preclude pregnancy termination by careful curettage.

350
00:50:40,688 --> 00:50:48,501
After the products are removed without trauma to the reproductive tract, full-dose heparin can be restarted in several hours.

351
00:50:48,501 --> 00:50:51,863
# Anticoagulation and Delivery.

352
00:50:51,863 --> 00:51:11,263
The effects of heparin on blood loss at delivery depend on several variables: (1) dose, route, and timing of administration; (2) number and depth of incisions and lacerations; (3) intensity of postpartum myometrial contractions; and (4) presence of other coagulation defects.

353
00:51:11,263 --> 00:51:19,226
Blood loss should not be greatly increased with vaginal delivery if there are no lacerations, and the uterus promptly contracts.

354
00:51:19,226 --> 00:51:24,151
Unfortunately, such ideal circumstances do not always prevail.

355
00:51:24,151 --> 00:51:28,976
Thus, heparin therapy generally is stopped during labor and delivery.

356
00:51:28,976 --> 00:51:44,026
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends restarting UFH or LMWH no sooner than 4 to 6 hours after vaginal delivery or 6 to 12 hours after cesarean delivery.

357
00:51:44,026 --> 00:51:54,338
From their review, Moriuchi and associates (2019) found that thromboprophylaxis started within 24 hours after cesarean delivery was safe.

358
00:51:54,338 --> 00:52:05,313
At Parkland Hospital, we wait at least 24 hours to restart therapeutic-dose heparin after cesarean delivery or after vaginal delivery with significant lacerations.

359
00:52:05,313 --> 00:52:08,488
Superficial Venous Thrombophlebitis.

360
00:52:08,488 --> 00:52:18,813
Thrombosis limited strictly to the superficial veins of the saphenous system is typically associated with varicosities or as a sequela of an indwelling intravenous catheter.

361
00:52:18,813 --> 00:52:23,851
It is treated with analgesia, elastic support, heat, and rest.

362
00:52:23,851 --> 00:52:29,301
Superficial vein thrombosis raises the risk of DVT four- to six-fold.

363
00:52:29,301 --> 00:52:39,163
In a metaanalysis of nonpregnant patients with thrombophlebitis, 18 percent had a concomitant DVT and 7 percent had a pulmonary embolism .

364
00:52:39,163 --> 00:52:45,926
Although controversial, the American Society of Hematology suggests LMWH therapy .

365
00:52:45,926 --> 00:52:48,138
Pulmonary Embolism.

366
00:52:48,138 --> 00:52:57,426
Although it causes approximately 10 percent of maternal deaths, pulmonary embolism is relatively uncommon during pregnancy and the puerperium.

367
00:52:57,426 --> 00:53:07,376
According to Marik and Plante (2008), 70 percent of gravidas presenting with a pulmonary embolism have associated clinical evidence of DVT.

368
00:53:07,376 --> 00:53:14,813
And recall that between 30 and 60 percent of women with a DVT will have a coexisting silent pulmonary embolism.

369
00:53:14,813 --> 00:53:17,638
# Clinical Presentation.

370
00:53:17,638 --> 00:53:34,076
In almost 2500 nonpregnant patients with a proven pulmonary embolism, symptoms included dyspnea in 82 percent, chest pain in 49 percent, cough in 20 percent, syncope in 14 percent, and hemoptysis in 7 percent .

371
00:53:34,076 --> 00:53:36,051
Other predominant

372
00:53:36,220 --> 00:53:41,957
clinical findings typically include tachypnea, apprehension, and tachycardia.

373
00:53:41,907 --> 00:53:48,820
In some cases, an accentuated pulmonic closure sound, rales, and/or friction rub is heard.

374
00:53:48,820 --> 00:53:56,220
Right axis deviation and T-wave inversion in the anterior chest leads may be evident on the electrocardiogram.

375
00:53:56,220 --> 00:54:00,445
In most cases, chest radiography results are normal.

376
00:54:00,445 --> 00:54:08,157
In others, nonspecific findings may include atelectasis, an infiltrate, cardiomegaly, or an effusion.

377
00:54:08,157 --> 00:54:13,607
Vascular markings in the lung region supplied by the obstructed artery can be lost.

378
00:54:13,607 --> 00:54:21,170
Although most women are hypoxemic, a normal arterial blood gas analysis does not exclude pulmonary embolism.

379
00:54:21,170 --> 00:54:27,595
Approximately a third of young patients have PO2 values >80 mm Hg.

380
00:54:27,595 --> 00:54:34,032
Thus, the alveolar- arterial oxygen tension difference is a more useful indicator of disease.

381
00:54:34,032 --> 00:54:43,070
More than 86 percent of patients with acute pulmonary embolism will have an alveolar-arterial difference >20 mm Hg .

382
00:54:43,070 --> 00:54:52,257
Even with massive pulmonary embolism, signs, symptoms, and laboratory data to support the diagnosis may be deceptively nonspecific.

383
00:54:52,257 --> 00:54:56,245
# Massive and Submassive Pulmonary Embolism.

384
00:54:56,245 --> 00:55:05,745
Defined as embolism causing hemodynamic instability, these account for 5 to 10 percent of cases (Handal-Örefice, 2019).

385
00:55:05,745 --> 00:55:16,195
Acute mechanical obstruction of the pulmonary vasculature causes increased vascular resistance and pulmonary hypertension followed by acute right ventricular dilation.

386
00:55:16,195 --> 00:55:26,420
In otherwise healthy patients, significant pulmonary hypertension does not develop until 60 to 75 percent of the pulmonary vascular tree is occluded .

387
00:55:26,420 --> 00:55:32,545
Moreover, circulatory collapse requires 75- to 80-percent obstruction.

388
00:55:32,545 --> 00:55:41,395
This is depicted in Figure 55-4 and emphasizes that most acutely symptomatic emboli are large and likely a saddle embolism .

389
00:55:41,395 --> 00:55:48,670
These are suspected when the pulmonary artery pressure is substantively increased as estimated by echocardiography.

390
00:55:48,670 --> 00:55:51,432
Figure 55-4.

391
00:55:51,432 --> 00:55:55,132
Schematic of pulmonary arterial circulation.

392
00:55:55,132 --> 00:56:02,207
Note that the cross-sectional area of the pulmonary trunk and the combined pulmonary arteries is 9 cm.

393
00:56:02,207 --> 00:56:10,107
A large saddle embolism could occlude 50 to 90 percent of the pulmonary tree, causing hemodynamic instability.

394
00:56:10,107 --> 00:56:29,407
As the arteries give off distal branches, the total surface area rapidly increases, that is, 13 cm for the combined five lobar arteries, 36 cm for the combined 19 segmental arteries, and more than 800 cm for the total 65 subsegmental arterial branches.

395
00:56:29,407 --> 00:56:35,570
Thus, hemodynamic instability is less likely with emboli past the lobar arteries.

396
00:56:35,570 --> 00:56:43,645
Submassive embolism is diagnosed when there is evidence of right ventricular dysfunction (Handal-Örefice, 2019).

397
00:56:43,645 --> 00:56:50,657
The mortality rate approaches 25 percent, which compares with a 1-percent rate without such dysfunction .

398
00:56:50,657 --> 00:56:57,507
It is important in these cases to infuse crystalloids carefully and to support blood pressure with vasopressors.

399
00:56:57,507 --> 00:57:04,307
Aggressive intravenous fluid infusion has been associated with worsening right-sided ventricular dysfunction .

400
00:57:04,307 --> 00:57:16,382
As discussed on page 988, oxygen treatment, tracheal intubation, and mechanical ventilation are completed preparatory to thrombolysis, filter placement, or embolectomy.

401
00:57:16,382 --> 00:57:18,782
# Diagnosis.

402
00:57:18,782 --> 00:57:26,957
In most cases, recognition of a pulmonary embolism requires a high index of suspicion that prompts objective evaluation.

403
00:57:26,957 --> 00:57:35,707
Exposure of the mother and fetus to ionizing radiation is a concern when investigating a suspected pulmonary embolism during pregnancy.

404
00:57:35,707 --> 00:57:42,507
However, this concern is largely overruled by the hazards of missing a potentially fatal diagnosis.

405
00:57:42,507 --> 00:57:50,295
Moreover, erroneously assigning a diagnosis of pulmonary embolism to a pregnant woman is also fraught with problems.

406
00:57:50,295 --> 00:58:02,945
It unnecessarily exposes the mother and fetus to the risks of anticoagulation treatment and will impact delivery plans, future contraception, and thromboprophylaxis during subsequent pregnancies.

407
00:58:02,945 --> 00:58:07,607
Therefore, investigations should aim for diagnostic certainty .

408
00:58:07,607 --> 00:58:15,207
Diagnosis of a pulmonary embolism follows the same algorithm as for DVT shown in Figure 55-2.

409
00:58:15,207 --> 00:58:24,795
In addition to compression ultrasound of the extremities, if a pulmonary embolism is suspected, chest x-ray and electrocardiogram may be revealing.

410
00:58:24,795 --> 00:58:28,382
Echocardiography is useful to detect other

411
00:58:28,540 --> 00:58:36,877
conditions that mimic pulmonary embolism—acute myocardial infarction, pericardial tamponade, and aortic dissection.

412
00:58:36,827 --> 00:58:46,415
Further imaging with computed tomography (CT) scanning, MR imaging, or ventilationperfusion lung scanning confirms the diagnosis .

413
00:58:46,415 --> 00:58:55,690
As discussed later (p. 988), the American Society of Hematology recommends lung scanning as the first-line diagnostic tool .

414
00:58:55,690 --> 00:59:03,015
At Parkland Hospital we preferentially use CT-pulmonary angiography for suspected pulmonary embolism.

415
00:59:03,015 --> 00:59:16,140
Computed Tomographic Pulmonary Angiography Multidetector CT-pulmonary angiography (CTPA) is the most commonly employed technique to diagnose pulmonary embolism in nonpregnant patients.

416
00:59:16,140 --> 00:59:25,790
The technique is described further in Chapter 49 (p. 874), and an imaging example is shown in Figure 55-5.

417
00:59:25,790 --> 00:59:32,977
The estimated fetal radiation exposure averages 0.45 to 0.6 mGy.

418
00:59:32,977 --> 00:59:37,690
The estimated maternal breast dose is 10 to 70 mGy .

419
00:59:37,690 --> 00:59:40,527
Figure 55-5.

420
00:59:40,527 --> 00:59:50,102
Axial image of the chest from a four-channel multidetector spiral computed tomographic scan performed after administration of intravenous contrast.

421
00:59:50,102 --> 00:59:58,052
There is enhancement of the pulmonary artery with a large thrombus on the right (arrow) consistent with pulmonary embolism.

422
00:59:58,052 --> 01:00:01,790
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Michael Landay.

423
01:00:01,790 --> 01:00:13,027
CTPA has many advantages, but we find that the higher resolution allows detection of previously inaccessible smaller distal emboli that have uncertain clinical significance.

424
01:00:13,027 --> 01:00:20,427
Similar observations have been reported by others (Anderson, 2007; Hall, 2009).

425
01:00:20,427 --> 01:00:31,840
Bourjeily and colleagues (2012) performed a follow-up study of 318 pregnant women who had a negative CTPA performed for a suspected pulmonary embolism.

426
01:00:31,840 --> 01:00:37,615
All were seen 3 months following their initial presentation or at 6 weeks postpartum.

427
01:00:37,615 --> 01:00:42,215
None of these women were subsequently diagnosed with a thromboembolism.

428
01:00:42,215 --> 01:00:53,502
Ventilation-Perfusion Scintigraphy Lung Scan This technique involves a small dose of radiotracer such as intravenously administered technetium-99m macroaggregated albumin.

429
01:00:53,502 --> 01:01:01,240
There is negligible fetal and maternal breast radiation exposure—0.1 to 0.4 mGy.

430
01:01:01,240 --> 01:01:08,052
The scan may not provide a definite diagnosis because many other conditions can cause perfusion defects.

431
01:01:08,052 --> 01:01:11,640
Examples are pneumonia or local bronchospasm.

432
01:01:11,640 --> 01:01:19,890
Chan and coworkers (2002) found that a fourth of ventilation-perfusion scans in pregnant women were nondiagnostic.

433
01:01:19,890 --> 01:01:23,565
In these instances, CTPA is preferred .

434
01:01:23,565 --> 01:01:35,652
To compare the performance of lung scintigraphy and CTPA, Revel and associates (2011) evaluated 137 pregnant women with suspected pulmonary embolism.

435
01:01:35,652 --> 01:01:45,027
The two modalities performed comparably and had no significant differences between the proportions of positive, negative, or indeterminate results.

436
01:01:45,027 --> 01:02:02,702
Specifically, the proportion of indeterminate results for both approximated 20 percent, Similarly, one systematic review concluded that both CTPA and lung scintigraphy seem appropriate for exclusion of pulmonary embolism during pregnancy (van Mens, 2017).

437
01:02:02,702 --> 01:02:12,440
Intravascular Pulmonary Angiography This requires catheterization of the right side of the heart and is considered the reference test for pulmonary embolism.

438
01:02:12,440 --> 01:02:20,927
With newer-generation multidetector CT scanners, however, the role of invasive pulmonary angiography has been questioned.

439
01:02:20,927 --> 01:02:25,827
This is especially true given the higher radiation exposure for the fetus .

440
01:02:25,827 --> 01:02:33,540
Other detractions are that it can be time consuming, uncomfortable, and associated with dyeinduced allergy and renal failure.

441
01:02:33,540 --> 01:02:38,415
It is reserved for confirmation when less invasive tests are equivocal.

442
01:02:38,415 --> 01:02:40,640
# Management.

443
01:02:40,640 --> 01:02:52,040
Immediate treatment for pulmonary embolism is full anticoagulation similar to that for DVT as discussed on page 982 and shown in Table 55-4.

444
01:02:52,040 --> 01:02:56,215
There are several complementary procedures that may be indicated.

445
01:02:56,215 --> 01:03:06,177
Vena Caval Filters The woman who has very recently suffered a pulmonary embolism and who must undergo cesarean delivery presents a particularly serious problem.

446
01:03:06,177 --> 01:03:17,640
Reversal of anticoagulation may be followed by another embolus, and surgery while fully anticoagulated frequently results in life-threatening hemorrhage or troublesome hematomas.

447
01:03:17,640 --> 01:03:22,502
In these cases, placement of a vena caval filter should be considered

448
01:03:22,660 --> 01:03:24,635
before surgery .

449
01:03:24,585 --> 01:03:40,410
Moreover, in the very infrequent circumstances in which heparin therapy fails to prevent recurrent pulmonary embolism from the pelvis or legs or in which embolism develops from these sites despite heparin treatment, a vena caval filter may also be indicated.

450
01:03:40,410 --> 01:03:47,235
Such filters can also be used following massive emboli in patients who are not candidates for thrombolysis.

451
01:03:47,235 --> 01:03:53,172
The device is inserted through either the jugular or femoral vein and can be inserted during labor .

452
01:03:53,172 --> 01:03:58,110
Routine filter placement has no added advantage to heparin given alone .

453
01:03:58,110 --> 01:04:04,222
Retrievable filters may be used as short-term protection and then removed 1 to 2 weeks later .

454
01:04:04,222 --> 01:04:16,022
From their systematic review, Harris and colleagues (2016) found that complication rates in pregnant women with vena caval filters are comparable to those in nonpregnant patients.

455
01:04:16,022 --> 01:04:24,760
In another series of 24 filters placed in obstetrical patients, 29 percent had complications including removal failure .

456
01:04:24,760 --> 01:04:39,172
Thrombolysis Compared with heparin, thrombolytic agents provide more rapid lysis of pulmonary clots and improvement of pulmonary hypertension in lifethreatening cases (Bates, 2021; Goldhaber, 2018).

457
01:04:39,172 --> 01:04:52,810
In one study of nonpregnant patients receiving heparin for an acute submassive pulmonary embolism, those receiving the recombinant tissue plasminogen activator alteplase had a lower risk of death or treatment escalation .

458
01:04:52,810 --> 01:05:07,372
One metaanalysis of trials involving nonpregnant patients reported that the risk of recurrence or death was significantly lower in patients given thrombolytic agents and heparin compared with those given heparin alone— 10 versus 17 percent .

459
01:05:07,372 --> 01:05:15,910
Importantly, however, there were five —2 percent—fatal bleeding episodes in the thrombolysis group and none in the heparin-only group.

460
01:05:15,910 --> 01:05:21,172
There have been several case reports and reviews of thrombolysis in pregnant women.

461
01:05:21,172 --> 01:05:29,910
Leonhardt and coworkers (2006) identified 28 reports of tissue plasminogen activator use during pregnancy.

462
01:05:29,910 --> 01:05:32,985
Ten cases were for thromboembolism.

463
01:05:32,985 --> 01:05:42,660
Complication rates were similar to those in nonpregnant patients, and the authors concluded that such therapy should not be withheld during pregnancy if indicated.

464
01:05:42,660 --> 01:05:53,085
Akazawa and Nishida (2017) reviewed 13 cases of systemic thrombolytic therapy administered during the first 48 hours after delivery.

465
01:05:53,085 --> 01:06:02,635
Blood transfusion was required in five of the eight cesarean deliveries, including three cases of hysterectomy and two cases of hematoma drainage.

466
01:06:02,635 --> 01:06:12,735
Sousa-Gomes and associates (2019) reviewed outcomes in 141 pregnant women undergoing thrombolysis for VTE or stroke.

467
01:06:12,735 --> 01:06:18,897
Maternal death complicated 3 percent of cases, and major bleeding occurred in 9 percent.

468
01:06:18,897 --> 01:06:26,247
Embolectomy Given the efficacy of thrombolysis and filters, surgical embolectomy is uncommonly indicated.

469
01:06:26,247 --> 01:06:36,622
Published experience with emergency embolectomy during pregnancy is limited to case reports (Colombier, 2015; Saeed, 2014).

470
01:06:36,622 --> 01:06:46,697
From their review, Ahearn and colleagues (2002) found that although the operative risk to the mother is reasonable, the stillbirth rate is 20 to 40 percent.

471
01:06:46,697 --> 01:06:48,997
Thromboprophylaxis.

472
01:06:48,997 --> 01:06:59,110
A Cochrane review of guidelines for thromboprophylaxis in pregnancy concluded that there is a lack of overall agreement about which women should be offered thromboprophylaxis .

473
01:06:59,110 --> 01:07:10,272
The American Society of Hematology summarized that evidence-based recommendations rely largely on observational studies and on data extrapolated from nonpregnant patients.

474
01:07:10,272 --> 01:07:17,485
In many cases, this led them to suggest—rather than recommend—various schemes for thromboprophylaxis .

475
01:07:17,485 --> 01:07:31,935
In an earlier study, Cleary-Goldman and coworkers (2007) surveyed 151 fellows of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and reported that intervention without a clear indication is common.

476
01:07:31,935 --> 01:07:38,360
Table 55-5 lists several consensus recommendations for thromboprophylaxis.

477
01:07:38,360 --> 01:07:45,110
In some cases, more than one option is listed, thus illustrating the confusion that currently reigns.

478
01:07:45,110 --> 01:07:59,160
It is important to emphasize that the decision to treat with anticoagulation in pregnancy is influenced by personal VTE history, family history of VTE, severity of thrombophilias, and additional risk factors such

479
01:07:59,308 --> 01:08:03,995
as obesity, cesarean delivery, or prolonged immobility .

480
01:08:03,945 --> 01:08:07,220
# Prior Venous Thromboembolism.

481
01:08:07,220 --> 01:08:16,395
In general, either antepartum surveillance or heparin prophylaxis is recommended for women with prior VTE but without a recurrent risk factor.

482
01:08:16,395 --> 01:08:23,620
Tengborn and associates (1989), however, suggested that such management may not be effective.

483
01:08:23,620 --> 01:08:32,133
They reported outcomes in 87 pregnant Swedish women who had prior thromboembolic disease and were not tested for thrombophilias.

484
01:08:32,133 --> 01:08:42,183
Despite unfractionated heparin prophylaxis, which was usually 5000 U twice daily, 15 percent developed antepartum VTE recurrence.

485
01:08:42,183 --> 01:08:45,833
This compared with 12 percent not given heparin.

486
01:08:45,833 --> 01:08:53,920
Brill-Edwards and colleagues (2000) prospectively studied 125 pregnant women with a single prior VTE.

487
01:08:53,920 --> 01:09:01,158
Antepartum heparin was not given, but anticoagulant therapy was given for 4 to 6 weeks postpartum.

488
01:09:01,158 --> 01:09:12,383
Six women had a recurrent venous thrombosis, but there were no recurrences in women without a known thrombophilia or whose prior thrombosis was associated with a temporary risk factor.

489
01:09:12,383 --> 01:09:24,758
A study of 88 women without antiphospholipid antibodies and who were not given antithrombotic prophylaxis reported a subsequent pregnancy- or puerperium-related VTE in 22 percent .

490
01:09:24,758 --> 01:09:38,033
Of 20 women whose original thrombosis was associated with a transient risk factor—not including pregnancy or oral contraceptive use—there were no recurrences during pregnancy, but two during the puerperium.

491
01:09:38,033 --> 01:09:50,583
These findings imply that women with a prior thrombosis in association with a thrombophilia or in the absence of a temporary risk factor generally should be given both antepartum and postpartum prophylaxis .

492
01:09:50,583 --> 01:10:01,408
These data also suggest that for women with a prior VTE, antithrombotic prophylaxis during pregnancy could be tailored according to the circumstances of the original event.

493
01:10:01,408 --> 01:10:07,658
It is important to emphasize that VTE may recur despite antithrombotic prophylaxis.

494
01:10:07,658 --> 01:10:15,458
A study of 270 women who had at least one previous VTE found that 10 percent suffered a recurrent VTE .

495
01:10:15,458 --> 01:10:26,495
Twelve of these recurrences occurred early in pregnancy before the initiation of antithrombotic prophylaxis, and 16 occurred despite prophylactic use of LMWH.

496
01:10:26,495 --> 01:10:38,795
Our practice at Parkland Hospital for many years for women with a history of prior VTE was to administer subcutaneous UFH, 5000 to 7500 units two to three times daily.

497
01:10:38,795 --> 01:10:47,483
More recently, we have used 40 mg of enoxaparin given subcutaneously daily with transition to UFH near delivery.

498
01:10:47,483 --> 01:10:53,445
With either regimen, the recurrence of documented DVT embolization has been uncommon.

499
01:10:53,445 --> 01:10:56,133
# Cesarean Delivery.

500
01:10:56,133 --> 01:11:05,583
The risk for DVT and especially for fatal thromboembolism rises many fold following cesarean delivery compared with that after vaginal delivery.

501
01:11:05,583 --> 01:11:21,183
When considering that a third of women giving birth in the United States yearly undergo cesarean delivery, pulmonary embolism is understandably a major cause of maternal mortality (Creanga, 2017; Petersen, 2019).

502
01:11:21,183 --> 01:11:41,508
That said, the lack of consensus described earlier by Middleton and coworkers (2021) creates considerable variation in the current recommendations promulgated by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, the Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists, and the American College of Chest Physicians .

503
01:11:41,508 --> 01:11:49,008
Adherence to individual society guidelines following cesarean delivery confers a greater risk in some instances.

504
01:11:49,008 --> 01:12:00,458
According to the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020), if American College guidelines were followed, only 1 percent of these women would be candidates for thromboprophylaxis.

505
01:12:00,458 --> 01:12:12,320
Almost 85 percent of the same cohort would be given thromboprophylaxis following Royal College guidelines and about 35 percent if American College of Chest Physician guidelines were followed.

506
01:12:12,320 --> 01:12:18,908
Some comparisons of these thromboprophylaxis guidelines are shown in Table 55-6.

507
01:12:18,908 --> 01:12:21,658
TABLE 55-6.

508
01:12:21,658 --> 01:12:40,570
Thromboprophylaxis Guidelines for Women Undergoing Cesarean Delivery Since 2011, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends placement of pneumatic compression devices before cesarean delivery for all women not already receiving thromboprophylaxis.

509
01:12:40,570 --> 01:12:42,995
This recommendation was

510
01:12:43,156 --> 01:12:47,118
based primarily on consensus and expert opinion.

511
01:12:47,068 --> 01:12:58,581
For patients undergoing cesarean delivery with additional risk factors for thromboembolism, both pneumatic compression devices and UFH or LMWH may be recommended.

512
01:12:58,581 --> 01:13:07,781
The College stipulated that cesarean delivery in an emergency setting should not be delayed because of the time necessary to begin thromboprophylaxis.

513
01:13:07,781 --> 01:13:32,906
Implementation of this strategy by the Hospital Corporation of America, the largest for-profit obstetrical health care delivery system in the United States, was associated with a reduction in deaths from pulmonary embolism from 7 of 458,097 cesarean births to 1 of 465,880 cesarean births (Clark, 2011, 2014).

514
01:13:32,906 --> 01:13:44,306
In 2016, the National Partnership for Maternal Safety published several consensus recommendations for the prevention of maternal VTE (D’Alton, 2016).

515
01:13:44,306 --> 01:14:02,043
These recommendations included expanded use of antenatal thromboprophylaxis for women hospitalized 3 days or longer, expanded use of prophylaxis during and after vaginal delivery, and expanded use of pharmacological prophylaxis to most women after cesarean delivery.

516
01:14:02,043 --> 01:14:13,481
In response, Sibai and Rouse (2016) expressed concern that these new recommendations derive from sparse data of questionable applicability to obstetrical patients.

517
01:14:13,481 --> 01:14:21,956
They called for better quality evidence to measure the benefits, harms, and costs of increased pharmacological thromboprophylaxis.

518
01:14:21,956 --> 01:14:39,768
As aptly expressed by Macones (2017), “an intervention, such as increased postcesarean pharmacologic thromboprophylaxis, where there are legitimate concerns about efficacy and safety, requires a much higher degree of evidence before a national guideline is implemented.

519
01:14:39,768 --> 01:14:42,231
We agree with these sentiments.

